      "text": "To determine the number of distinct sequences of four letters that can be made from the letters in \"EQUALS\" with the constraints that each sequence must begin with \"L\" and end with \"Q\" and no letter can appear more than once, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the available letters after eliminating the first letter \"L\" and the last letter \"Q\": \"E\", \"U\", \"A\", \"S\".\n2. We need to form a sequence of four letters from these five letters, where one of the letters is \"E\", one of the letters is \"U\", one of the letters is \"A\", and one of the letters is \"S\". Therefore, we need to choose 2 out of these 4 letters to complete the sequence.\n3. The number of ways to choose 2 letters out of 4 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{4}{2} \\). This is calculated as follows:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{4}{2} = \\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!} = \\frac{4!}{2!2!} = \\frac{4 \\times 3}{2 \\times 1} = 6\n   \\]\n4. For each choice of 2 letters, there is exactly one way to arrange them in a sequence of 4 positions. Therefore, the number of distinct sequences is simply the number of ways to choose the 2 letters, which is 6.\n\nThus, the number of distinct sequences of four letters that can be made under the given conditions is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
