      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of distinct sequences of four letters that can be made from the letters in \"EQUALS\" such that the sequence begins with \"L\" and ends with \"Q\", and no letter can be repeated.\n\nGiven the letters in \"EQUALS\" are: E, Q, U, L, A, S, the distinct positions we need to fill are:\n\n1. First position: Fixed as \"L\".\n2. Second position: Can be any of the remaining letters except L and Q (i.e., E, U, A, S).\n3. Third position: Can be any of the remaining letters except L, Q, and the letter already used in the second position.\n4. Fourth position: Can be any of the remaining letters except L, Q, and the letter already used in the third position.\n\nFirst, let's choose the second letter. We have 4 choices (E, U, A, S).\n\nOnce the second letter is chosen, we have 4 letters left to choose from for the third position (since one letter has already been used), and of those, 3 will be used, so we have 3 choices left for the third position.\n\nFinally, the fourth letter is determined by the letters already used. After choosing the second, third, and fourth letters, the fourth letter is fixed because it must be a specific character to complete our sequence.\n\nThus, the total number of distinct sequences is calculated as:\n\\[ 4 \\text{ (choices for the second letter)} \\times 3 \\text{ (choices for the third letter)} \\times 1 \\text{ (choice for the fourth letter)} = 4 \\times 3 = 12. \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct sequences of four letters that can be made is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
